from (values
  (1, 'Diagnostic: where are you now?', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"Organizational behavior studies human behavior at which levels?","options":["only the individual","only the group","the individual, the group, and the organization","only senior executives"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The field is organized around three nested levels of analysis - the individual, the group, and the organization."},{"prompt":"The Hawthorne studies are best known for showing that...","options":["pay is the only real motivator","attention and social factors can affect worker output","lighting has no effect on anything","workers prefer to work in isolation"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Output rose under almost any change, which pointed to the power of attention and social meaning rather than physical conditions alone."},{"prompt":"Maslow''s hierarchy of needs places which need at the base?","options":["esteem","self-actualization","physiological needs","belonging"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Basic physiological needs sit at the bottom and tend to dominate until they are met."},{"prompt":"A transformational leader mainly...","options":["avoids all change","inspires followers toward a shared vision","relies only on punishment","ignores followers'' needs"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Transformational leaders lift followers through vision, inspiration, challenge, and personal attention."},{"prompt":"Job satisfaction is best described as...","options":["a personality trait fixed at birth","an attitude reflecting how one feels about one''s job","the same thing as salary","a type of conflict"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Job satisfaction is a work attitude - a positive or negative evaluation of one''s job."},{"prompt":"Equity theory says people compare...","options":["their height to that of others","their input-to-outcome ratio to that of others","their age to the company''s age","nothing at all"],"answer":1,"explanation":"People weigh their own ratio of inputs to outcomes against others and act to restore fairness."},{"prompt":"Organizational culture refers to...","options":["the building''s architecture","shared values and assumptions that guide behavior","the annual budget","only the legal structure"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Culture is the shared values, beliefs, and assumptions that shape how members behave."},{"prompt":"Functional conflict is conflict that...","options":["always harms the group","can improve decisions and performance","is always about personality","must always be eliminated"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Moderate task-focused conflict can sharpen thinking and improve decisions."}]}'::jsonb, null, 10),
  (2, 'What organizational behavior studies: individual, group, and organization', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Define organizational behavior and explain why managers study it. Describe the three levels at which behavior is analyzed - the individual, the group, and the organization - and give an example at each level. Explain how the field draws on psychology, sociology, and economics to make workplaces both more effective and more humane, rather than relying on hunch or fashion. You will also be able to spot when a problem that looks personal is really a group norm or a flaw in how the organization is designed. Warm-up: think of one workplace problem you have seen, and ask whether it lives mainly inside a person, inside a team, or inside the wider organization. Holding that example in mind will make the rest of the course concrete, because every idea here attaches to at least one of these three levels."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Organizational behavior is the systematic study of how people act, think, and feel inside organizations, and how organizations act in turn. It treats the workplace as something you can observe, measure, and improve rather than merely guess about. The field is usually split into three levels of analysis. The individual level looks at one person: personality, perception, motivation, and attitudes. The group level looks at how two or more people interact: teams, norms, roles, and communication. The organizational level looks at the whole system: structure, culture, and change. These levels nest inside one another, so a problem that looks personal may really be a group norm or a design flaw in the organization. Skilled managers move between levels instead of blaming individuals for system faults. The discipline borrows from psychology for the individual level, from social psychology and sociology for the group and organization levels, and from economics and political science for incentives and power."},{"heading":"Key theories & examples","text":"The modern field grew out of the Hawthorne studies, carried out at a Western Electric plant near Chicago between 1924 and 1932. Researchers expected that brighter lighting would raise output. Instead output rose under almost every change, and even when conditions were returned to normal. The lesson, often called the Hawthorne effect, is that workers responded to the attention and social meaning of being studied, not only to physical conditions. This finding helped launch the human relations movement, which argued that social factors and group belonging shape productivity as much as pay or equipment. A second anchor is evidence-based management, the practice of grounding decisions in careful data rather than fashion. Example across levels: if sales fall, the individual view asks about skill and motivation, the group view asks about team norms and coordination, and the organizational view asks about structure, rewards, and culture. Each lens suggests a different fix, and the best diagnosis often checks all three."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Name the three levels of analysis in organizational behavior. 2) At which level would you study a team''s unwritten rules? 3) What did the Hawthorne studies suggest about attention and output? 4) Which discipline feeds the individual level most directly? 5) Give an organizational-level cause of falling sales. Answers: 1) the individual, the group, and the organization. 2) the group level, where norms live. 3) that being observed and valued can raise output on its own, the pattern named the Hawthorne effect. 4) mainly psychology, which studies personality, perception, and motivation. 5) a reward system or structure that does not support selling, or a culture that discourages initiative - each is a system-level cause rather than a personal one."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: treating every problem as a personal failing when it is really a group norm or a structural design choice. Mistake: assuming the Hawthorne studies proved that lighting raises output - they showed the opposite, that social attention mattered more than the lighting itself. Mistake: believing organizational behavior is just common sense, when careful study often overturns confident guesses about what motivates people. Exit: (a) What are the three levels of analysis? (b) State the Hawthorne effect in one sentence. Answers: the individual, the group, and the organization; the Hawthorne effect is the tendency for people to change their behavior, often raising output, simply because they are being observed and given attention."}]}'::jsonb, 'Keep the three levels straight: a problem may live at the individual level, the group level, or the organizational level, and each often needs a different fix.', 13),
  (3, 'Individual differences: personality, perception, attitudes, and emotions', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Describe the main individual differences that shape behavior at work: personality, perception, attitudes, and emotions. Use the Big Five model to talk about personality in a structured way instead of with loose labels. Explain how perception can distort judgment through predictable bias, and how attitudes such as job satisfaction and organizational commitment predict what people actually do. Tell the difference between an emotion and a mood, and say why both matter for effort and decisions. Warm-up: recall a time when two coworkers saw the same event very differently, and ask what in each person shaped that reading. By the end you should be able to explain a workplace disagreement partly in terms of perception and attitude rather than treating one person as simply right and the other as wrong."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"People differ in stable ways, and those differences predict behavior. Personality is the set of enduring traits that make a person recognizable over time. The most widely used map is the Big Five: openness, conscientiousness, extraversion, agreeableness, and neuroticism, often remembered as OCEAN. Conscientiousness is the single best trait predictor of job performance across most roles. Perception is how we select, organize, and interpret what we sense; because we cannot process everything, we take shortcuts that sometimes mislead us. Attitudes are evaluations, favorable or unfavorable, about people, objects, or events, and they have thinking, feeling, and behaving parts. Two job attitudes matter most: job satisfaction, how positive a person feels about their work, and organizational commitment, how attached they feel to the employer. Emotions are short, intense reactions to a specific event, while moods are milder and longer lasting; both color decisions and effort."},{"heading":"Key theories & examples","text":"The Big Five emerged from decades of trait research and holds up across cultures and languages. Perception errors are well documented: the halo effect lets one good trait color our whole view of a person; stereotyping judges someone by a group label; and the fundamental attribution error leads us to blame a person''s character for behavior while excusing our own as situational. Attitudes and behavior can drift apart, and Leon Festinger''s theory of cognitive dissonance explains the discomfort people feel when actions and beliefs clash, which pushes them to change one or the other. On emotions, Arlie Hochschild described emotional labor, the effort of managing feelings to meet job demands, such as a service worker staying cheerful under stress. Example: a manager who rates a quiet but highly conscientious employee poorly may be falling for the halo effect around a louder, more extraverted peer. Measuring attitudes through anonymous surveys helps catch such distortions before they harden into unfair decisions."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) List the Big Five traits. 2) Which trait best predicts job performance across roles? 3) Name the bias where one good trait colors the whole impression. 4) Define job satisfaction in one line. 5) What is emotional labor? 6) What does cognitive dissonance describe? Answers: 1) openness, conscientiousness, extraversion, agreeableness, and neuroticism. 2) conscientiousness. 3) the halo effect. 4) how positive a person feels about their job. 5) the effort of managing one''s feelings to meet job demands. 6) the discomfort of holding clashing beliefs and actions, which drives a person to change one of them to restore consistency."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: treating personality as destiny, when traits set tendencies rather than fixed outcomes and the situation also matters. Mistake: trusting a first impression as objective fact when the halo effect and stereotyping quietly shape it. Mistake: confusing emotions, which are brief and event-driven, with moods, which are milder and longer lasting. Exit: (a) What does OCEAN stand for? (b) Name one perception bias and say what it does. Answers: openness, conscientiousness, extraversion, agreeableness, and neuroticism; the halo effect, which lets a single positive trait sway the overall judgment of a person - stereotyping and the fundamental attribution error are also acceptable answers."}]}'::jsonb, 'Traits set tendencies, not certainties; always pair a claim about someone''s personality with the situation that person is in.', 14),
  (4, 'Motivation: needs, fairness, and goals', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain what motivation is and compare the major theories managers use to raise it. Distinguish Maslow''s hierarchy of needs, Herzberg''s two-factor theory, expectancy theory, equity theory, and goal-setting theory, and say when each is most useful. Separate content theories, which ask what people want, from process theories, which ask how motivation actually works. Choose a practical motivator for a given situation rather than reaching for money by reflex. Warm-up: think of a task you did with real energy and one you dragged yourself through, and ask what differed between them. Often the difference was not the reward but whether you believed your effort would pay off, thought the goal was clear, and felt the outcome was fair - exactly the levers these theories describe."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Motivation is the set of forces that start, direct, and sustain effort toward a goal. Content theories ask what people want. Abraham Maslow arranged needs in a hierarchy - physiological, safety, belonging, esteem, and self-actualization - and argued that lower needs dominate until they are met. Frederick Herzberg split work factors in two: hygiene factors such as pay, policy, and conditions, which cause dissatisfaction when poor but do not motivate when good, and motivators such as achievement, recognition, and growth, which drive real engagement. Process theories ask how motivation works. Victor Vroom''s expectancy theory says effort depends on three beliefs: that effort will produce performance, that performance will bring a reward, and that the reward is valued. J. Stacy Adams''s equity theory says people compare their ratio of inputs to outcomes with that of others and act to restore fairness. Edwin Locke and Gary Latham''s goal-setting theory shows that specific, challenging, accepted goals with feedback beat vague do-your-best urging."},{"heading":"Key theories & examples","text":"Evidence favors the process theories. Goal-setting is among the best-supported ideas in the field: a target such as raise the response rate to ninety percent in sixty days outperforms try harder, provided the person accepts it and gets feedback. Expectancy theory explains why a rich bonus fails when staff doubt they can hit the number, or do not value the reward on offer. Equity theory explains quiet resentment: a strong performer who learns a weaker peer earns more may cut effort, ask for a raise, or leave, all to rebalance the ratio. Herzberg warns that fixing only hygiene factors - a pay bump, a nicer office - buys calm, not drive; lasting motivation needs genuine achievement and recognition. Maslow''s neat pyramid has weak research support as a strict order, yet it remains a useful reminder that unmet basic needs crowd out higher ones. Example: to motivate a tired team, set clear reachable goals, tie valued rewards to results, keep pay fair, and build in recognition."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Name Maslow''s five levels from the bottom up. 2) Give one hygiene factor and one motivator from Herzberg''s theory. 3) State the three beliefs in expectancy theory. 4) What comparison drives equity theory? 5) What makes a goal effective under goal-setting theory? Answers: 1) physiological, safety, belonging, esteem, and self-actualization. 2) a hygiene factor is pay or conditions; a motivator is achievement or recognition. 3) that effort leads to performance, that performance leads to a reward, and that the reward is valued. 4) one''s own input-to-outcome ratio versus that of others. 5) it is specific, challenging, accepted, and paired with feedback rather than vague."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: assuming money motivates everyone equally, when expectancy theory shows a reward works only if it is believed in and valued. Mistake: treating Maslow''s order as strict law, when the evidence for a fixed sequence is weak. Mistake: setting vague goals like do your best, which perform worse than specific, challenging targets with feedback. Exit: (a) In Herzberg''s theory, do hygiene factors motivate, or only prevent dissatisfaction? (b) Name the three links in expectancy theory. Answers: hygiene factors prevent dissatisfaction but do not motivate; the three links are effort to performance, performance to reward, and the value of the reward to the person."}]}'::jsonb, 'Content theories ask what people want; process theories ask how motivation works - name which kind you are using before you apply it.', 14),
  (5, 'Checkpoint 1', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"The three levels of analysis in organizational behavior are...","options":["cost, revenue, and profit","the individual, the group, and the organization","past, present, and future","input, process, and output"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The field nests the individual inside the group inside the organization."},{"prompt":"Which Big Five trait is the best single predictor of job performance across roles?","options":["extraversion","openness","conscientiousness","neuroticism"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Conscientiousness - being organized, dependable, and achievement-focused - predicts performance most broadly."},{"prompt":"In Herzberg''s two-factor theory, which is a hygiene factor?","options":["recognition","achievement","working conditions and pay","personal growth"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Hygiene factors like pay and conditions prevent dissatisfaction but do not themselves motivate."},{"prompt":"Expectancy theory says motivation requires...","options":["only a large reward","belief that effort leads to performance and to a valued reward","equal pay for everyone","a strict hierarchy of needs"],"answer":1,"explanation":"All three beliefs must hold: effort to performance, performance to reward, and the reward must be valued."},{"prompt":"Cognitive dissonance is...","options":["the comfort of aligned beliefs","the discomfort felt when actions and beliefs clash","a stable personality trait","a kind of financial reward"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The discomfort pushes a person to change an attitude or a behavior to restore consistency."},{"prompt":"The Hawthorne effect describes how...","options":["lighting alone raises output","people change behavior when observed and given attention","pay is the only motivator","workers ignore researchers entirely"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Output rose because of the attention and social meaning of being studied, not the physical changes alone."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8),
  (6, 'Groups, teams, and communication', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain how groups form and how they turn into effective teams. Use Tuckman''s stages to track a team''s development over time, and name the conditions that make teams perform well. Describe the basic communication process and the common barriers that distort it. Recognize two classic group traps - social loafing and groupthink - and name a remedy for each. Warm-up: recall a team that clicked and one that never did, and list one concrete difference you noticed between them. That difference is usually not talent but something structural: a clear goal, visible contributions, agreed norms, or honest communication. This lesson gives you the vocabulary to name what went right or wrong and to design the conditions that help the next team succeed."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"A group is two or more people who interact and depend on one another; a team is a group with complementary skills and shared accountability for a result. Bruce Tuckman described five stages most groups pass through: forming, where members are polite and unsure; storming, where conflict over roles and goals surfaces; norming, where shared expectations settle; performing, where the team works smoothly; and adjourning, where it disbands. Groups develop norms, the unwritten rules of acceptable behavior, and assign roles, the expected patterns for each position. Cohesion, the glue that holds members together, raises satisfaction and, when paired with high performance norms, raises output too. Communication is the transfer and understanding of meaning. The basic model runs from a sender who encodes a message, through a channel, to a receiver who decodes it, with feedback closing the loop. Noise - anything that distorts the message - can enter at any point along the way."},{"heading":"Key theories & examples","text":"Effective teams share a few conditions: a clear and compelling goal, the right size and mix of skills, mutual trust, and real accountability. Research on process loss warns that adding people can lower per-person output; social loafing is the tendency to coast when individual effort is hidden in a group, and it shrinks when contributions are visible and roles are clear. Groupthink, named by Irving Janis, is the drive for agreement that suppresses dissent and produces poor decisions; remedies include inviting a devil''s advocate and protecting minority views. On communication, barriers include filtering, where a sender shades a message to please the receiver, information overload, selective perception, and poor listening. Rich channels such as face-to-face talk carry tone and allow instant feedback, so they suit complex or sensitive messages, while lean channels such as a short memo suit simple, routine ones. Example: a team stuck in storming can move forward by naming roles and agreeing on norms rather than avoiding the friction."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Name Tuckman''s five stages in order. 2) What is social loafing? 3) Define a group norm. 4) What is groupthink, and name one remedy. 5) List the parts of the basic communication model. 6) Which channel suits a complex, sensitive message? Answers: 1) forming, storming, norming, performing, and adjourning. 2) coasting when one''s effort is hidden within the group. 3) an unwritten rule of acceptable behavior in the group. 4) the push for agreement that silences dissent; a remedy is appointing a devil''s advocate. 5) sender, encoding, channel, receiver, decoding, and feedback, with noise able to enter at any point. 6) a rich channel such as a face-to-face conversation."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: skipping storming and hoping conflict disappears on its own, when naming roles and norms resolves it faster than silence. Mistake: assuming bigger teams always do more, when process loss and social loafing can cut total output. Mistake: using a lean channel such as a quick message for a sensitive issue that needs tone and feedback. Exit: (a) Which Tuckman stage involves conflict over roles and goals? (b) Name one barrier to communication. Answers: storming; filtering, information overload, selective perception, or poor listening are all acceptable barriers that distort the intended meaning."}]}'::jsonb, 'A group becomes a team when it shares complementary skills and joint accountability, not just a room or a meeting slot.', 13),
  (7, 'Leadership: from traits to transformation', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Trace how thinking about leadership moved from traits to behaviors to situations to transformation. Compare trait, behavioral, contingency, and transformational theories, and explain what each one adds that the earlier view missed. Tell the difference between a transactional and a transformational leader, and say why the best leaders combine them. Recognize the romance of leadership, the habit of over-crediting leaders for outcomes shaped by many forces. Warm-up: picture the best leader you have worked with and jot one word for what made them effective. Hold that word up against each theory as you read: does it name a trait, a behavior, a fit to the situation, or the power to inspire a shared vision? The answer usually touches more than one, which is the point of the lesson."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Leadership is the ability to influence a group toward goals, and it is not the same as formal authority. Early trait theories searched for the qualities that set leaders apart, such as drive, confidence, and intelligence; traits matter but do not guarantee success. Behavioral theories then asked what leaders do rather than who they are. The Ohio State studies found two broad behaviors: initiating structure, which organizes work and roles, and consideration, which shows concern for people. Contingency theories added that the best style depends on the situation. Fred Fiedler argued that a leader''s style is fairly fixed and must be matched to how favorable the situation is. Robert House''s path-goal theory says leaders should clear the path to goals by adjusting their style to followers and tasks. The newest wave distinguishes transactional leaders, who exchange rewards for performance, from transformational leaders, who raise followers'' sights through vision, inspiration, intellectual challenge, and personal attention."},{"heading":"Key theories & examples","text":"Transformational leadership, developed by Bernard Bass from James MacGregor Burns''s ideas, is among the most studied styles and is linked to higher commitment and performance. Its four parts are often called the four I''s: idealized influence, inspirational motivation, intellectual stimulation, and individualized consideration. Transactional leadership still matters; the strongest leaders use both, setting clear exchanges and then lifting people beyond them. Contingency thinking shows why a style that works in a calm, structured unit can fail in a crisis. Example: a new leader of a struggling team might first use transactional clarity - clear targets and fair rewards - then build transformational trust through a shared vision and attention to each member. Evidence also warns against the romance of leadership, the habit of crediting leaders for outcomes that owe more to markets, teams, or luck. Measuring results rather than charisma keeps judgment honest and helps organizations promote the leaders who truly add value."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What two behaviors did the Ohio State studies identify? 2) What is the core claim of contingency theories? 3) Name the four I''s of transformational leadership. 4) How does a transactional leader mainly operate? 5) What is the romance of leadership? Answers: 1) initiating structure and consideration. 2) that the best leadership style depends on the situation. 3) idealized influence, inspirational motivation, intellectual stimulation, and individualized consideration. 4) by exchanging rewards for performance. 5) the tendency to over-credit leaders for results that were shaped by many forces beyond their control, such as markets and luck."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: hunting for a single set of leader traits as if context did not matter. Mistake: treating transformational and transactional styles as rivals, when the best leaders blend them. Mistake: crediting a leader for every good or bad result while ignoring markets, teams, and luck. Exit: (a) Which family of theories says the right style depends on the situation? (b) Name two of the four I''s. Answers: contingency theories; any two of idealized influence, inspirational motivation, intellectual stimulation, and individualized consideration count as correct."}]}'::jsonb, 'Transactional leaders trade rewards for results; transformational leaders raise followers'' sights - the strongest leaders use both together.', 13),
  (8, 'Power, conflict, and organizational culture', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Name the sources of power and the tactics people use to influence others. Explain how conflict can help or harm a group, and how negotiation resolves it. Define organizational culture and describe how it is created, embedded, and sustained over time. Tell functional conflict apart from dysfunctional conflict, and distributive bargaining apart from integrative bargaining. Warm-up: think of someone at work who had real influence without a big title, and ask where their power came from. The answer is almost always expert or referent power - knowledge or admiration - rather than position. This lesson gives you a map of power, a way to think about conflict as sometimes useful, and a model of culture as something leaders build through what they attend to, reward, and model every day."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Power is the capacity to affect the behavior of others. John French and Bertram Raven named five bases: legitimate power from position, reward power from giving valued things, coercive power from punishment, expert power from knowledge, and referent power from being admired. Expert and referent power, which rest on the person rather than the post, tend to earn genuine commitment rather than mere compliance. Influence tactics include rational persuasion, inspirational appeals, consultation, and, less effectively, raw pressure. Conflict is perceived opposition of interests. Not all conflict is bad: functional conflict over tasks and ideas can sharpen decisions, while dysfunctional conflict, often personal, drains the group. Organizational culture is the shared values, beliefs, and assumptions that shape how members behave - the way things are done here. It is learned, largely taken for granted, and passed on through stories, rituals, symbols, and the examples that leaders set day after day."},{"heading":"Key theories & examples","text":"Edgar Schein described culture in three layers: visible artifacts such as dress and office layout; espoused values such as a stated mission; and deep underlying assumptions that members rarely question. Culture is founded by leaders, embedded through what they attend to, reward, and model, and sustained by selection, socialization, and story. On conflict, researchers distinguish task conflict, useful in moderate amounts, from relationship conflict, which is almost always harmful. Negotiation offers two broad approaches: distributive bargaining divides a fixed pie and tends toward win-lose, while integrative bargaining expands the pie by trading on differing priorities and tends toward win-win. Example: two departments fighting over a shrinking budget can shift from distributive demands to an integrative search for shared goals and traded concessions. A strong culture aligns behavior without heavy rules, yet it can also resist needed change, which is why culture features so heavily in the study of organizational change."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Name French and Raven''s five bases of power. 2) Which two rest on the person rather than the position? 3) What is functional conflict? 4) Name Schein''s three layers of culture. 5) How does integrative bargaining differ from distributive bargaining? Answers: 1) legitimate, reward, coercive, expert, and referent power. 2) expert and referent power. 3) conflict over tasks and ideas that can improve decisions. 4) artifacts, espoused values, and underlying assumptions. 5) integrative bargaining expands the pie for a win-win, while distributive bargaining divides a fixed pie toward a win-lose outcome."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: thinking power comes only from a job title, when expert and referent power often matter more. Mistake: trying to stamp out all conflict, when moderate task conflict can actually improve decisions. Mistake: reading culture only from posters and slogans while ignoring the deep assumptions beneath them. Exit: (a) Which power base rests on admiration? (b) What is the difference between task conflict and relationship conflict? Answers: referent power; task conflict is about the work and can help in moderation, while relationship conflict is personal and usually harms the group."}]}'::jsonb, 'Power from the person, such as expert and referent power, earns commitment; power from the post, such as legitimate, reward, and coercive power, often earns only compliance.', 14),
  (9, 'Key terms', 'vocab', '{"items":[{"term":"organizational behavior","translation":"the systematic study of how people and groups act within organizations, and how organizations behave in turn","example":"A manager used organizational behavior to redesign a struggling team''s roles and rewards."},{"term":"Hawthorne effect","translation":"the tendency of people to change their behavior, often raising output, when they know they are being observed","example":"Output rose during the study partly because of the Hawthorne effect, not the lighting."},{"term":"Big Five (OCEAN)","translation":"the five-trait model of personality: openness, conscientiousness, extraversion, agreeableness, and neuroticism","example":"On the Big Five, she scored high in conscientiousness, which predicts strong performance."},{"term":"job satisfaction","translation":"a positive or negative attitude that reflects how a person feels about their job","example":"Fair pay and supportive managers lifted job satisfaction across the whole branch."},{"term":"expectancy theory","translation":"the view that effort depends on believing it leads to performance, that performance brings a reward, and that the reward is valued","example":"Expectancy theory explained why a generous bonus still failed to motivate the team."},{"term":"group cohesion","translation":"the degree to which members are attracted to a group and motivated to stay in it","example":"High cohesion combined with strong performance norms raised the team''s output."},{"term":"transformational leadership","translation":"a style that lifts followers through vision, inspiration, intellectual challenge, and personal attention","example":"Her transformational leadership turned a skeptical group into committed believers."},{"term":"organizational culture","translation":"the shared values, beliefs, and assumptions that guide how members of an organization behave","example":"A culture of openness made it safe to report mistakes early rather than hide them."}]}'::jsonb, null, 10),
  (10, 'Organizational change and development', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Explain why organizations must change and why people resist it. Use Kurt Lewin''s three-step model and John Kotter''s eight steps to plan a change effort that actually sticks. Describe organizational development as a planned, evidence-based approach to improving an organization''s effectiveness and health. Treat resistance as information rather than mere stubbornness, and name the strongest cure for it. Warm-up: recall a change at work or school that failed, and ask what the people leading it missed. Most failed changes skip a step that this lesson will name: they never built urgency, never involved the people affected, or declared victory too early and let old habits return. By the end you will have a simple checklist for leading change that respects both the goal and the people who must live with it."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Organizations face constant pressure to change from technology, competition, regulation, and a shifting workforce. Change can be incremental, a steady series of small adjustments, or transformational, a sharp break with the past. People resist change for understandable reasons: fear of loss, uncertainty, habit, distrust of those leading it, and a sense that the change threatens their interests. Resistance is not simply stubbornness; it often carries useful information about real risks. Kurt Lewin offered a simple, durable model with three steps: unfreeze the current state by building a case for change and loosening old habits; move to the new state through new practices and support; and refreeze by locking in the change so it becomes the new normal. Lewin also used force-field analysis, which maps the driving forces pushing for change against the restraining forces holding it back, and advises weakening the restraints rather than only adding more pressure."},{"heading":"Key theories & examples","text":"John Kotter expanded the idea into eight steps, beginning with creating a sense of urgency, building a guiding coalition, and forming a clear vision, then communicating it widely, empowering action, generating short-term wins, consolidating gains, and anchoring the change in the culture. His central warning is that leaders declare victory too soon and let old habits return. Organizational development, often shortened to OD, is a planned, organization-wide effort, guided by behavioral science, to improve effectiveness and health; its methods include survey feedback, team building, and process consultation, and it stresses involving the people affected. Involvement is the strongest known cure for resistance: people support what they help build. Example: a company adopting new software succeeds not by announcing it but by explaining the urgency, training staff, winning early supporters, celebrating quick wins, and adjusting based on feedback. Change that skips these steps tends to stall as soon as the initial push fades."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Name Lewin''s three steps of change. 2) What does force-field analysis map? 3) Give two honest reasons people resist change. 4) What is Kotter''s first step? 5) What is the strongest cure for resistance? Answers: 1) unfreeze, move or change, and refreeze. 2) the driving forces pushing for change against the restraining forces holding it back. 3) fear of loss and uncertainty - habit and distrust of leaders are also acceptable. 4) creating a sense of urgency. 5) involving the people affected so that they help build the change and come to own it."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: treating all resistance as irrational, when it often flags real risks worth hearing. Mistake: skipping the unfreeze step and pushing a change before people see the need for it. Mistake: declaring victory too early, which lets old habits creep back before the change is anchored in the culture. Exit: (a) What are Lewin''s three steps? (b) Why should you involve affected employees in a change? Answers: unfreeze, move, and refreeze; because people support what they help create, which lowers resistance and improves the design of the change itself."}]}'::jsonb, 'Unfreeze before you move: people accept a change once they see the need for it, not before, so build the case first.', 15)
) as v(pos, title, kind, body, notes, mins)
